WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.089 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A school garden needs a fence.

00:00:02.309 --> 00:00:06.229 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Would counting the squares inside tell us how much fencing
to buy?

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:09.555 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine walking all the way around its edge.

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:13.139 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That journey measures the boundary, which is called the
perimeter.

00:00:13.789 --> 00:00:18.138 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to find the perimeter of regular and
irregular shapes.

00:00:18.788 --> 00:00:20.545 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:20.765 --> 00:00:23.603 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can trace the complete outside boundary.

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:26.046 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can use matching length units.

00:00:26.266 --> 00:00:29.444 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can find missing lengths before adding sides.

00:00:30.094 --> 00:00:33.944 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use string to trace a shape, then straighten the string
beside a ruler.

00:00:34.164 --> 00:00:37.015 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This makes the idea of perimeter concrete.

00:00:37.235 --> 00:00:42.727 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On a diagram, follow every outside side once and add those
lengths using matching units.

00:00:42.947 --> 00:00:47.043 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An inside dividing line is not part of the outside boundary.

00:00:47.693 --> 00:00:52.131 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rectangular garden is seven metres long and four metres
wide.

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:54.820 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Opposite sides have matching lengths.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.730 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Trace the four edges and predict the total before we add
them.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.510 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Seven plus four plus seven plus four is twenty-two metres.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.018 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can also add seven and four, then double eleven.

00:01:07.238 --> 00:01:10.691 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both methods count the complete boundary exactly once.

00:01:11.341 --> 00:01:13.518 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now trace an L-shaped garden.

00:01:13.738 --> 00:01:17.260 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its top is eight metres and its left side is six.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:21.491 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A three-by-two rectangle is missing from the lower right
corner.

00:01:21.711 --> 00:01:25.051 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use subtraction to find the remaining straight lengths.

00:01:25.701 --> 00:01:30.820 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The six outside edges measure eight, four, three, two, five
and six metres.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.342 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their sum is twenty-eight metres.

00:01:33.562 --> 00:01:38.802 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The inward corner still belongs to the outside boundary, so
include both of its edges.

00:01:39.452 --> 00:01:43.265 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Seven times four counts square units covering this
rectangle.

00:01:43.485 --> 00:01:45.472 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It does not measure the fence.

00:01:45.692 --> 00:01:48.831 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For perimeter, trace and add the outside lengths.

00:01:49.051 --> 00:01:53.095 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A useful check is whether your answer has metres or square
metres.

00:01:53.745 --> 00:01:56.987 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rectangle is six metres by three metres.

00:01:57.207 --> 00:01:58.854 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What is its perimeter?

00:02:02.854 --> 00:02:05.041 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six plus three makes nine.

00:02:05.261 --> 00:02:08.628 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Double nine for the four sides: eighteen metres.

00:02:08.848 --> 00:02:12.651 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check by tracing and adding six, three, six and three.

00:02:13.301 --> 00:02:16.853 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Perimeter is a length around the complete outside edge.

00:02:17.073 --> 00:02:21.655 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Trace before calculating, find hidden lengths and keep units
consistent.

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:27.435 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Try outlining a book with string, then compare a direct
measurement with the total of its side lengths.

00:02:27.655 --> 00:02:30.485 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and why.

00:02:30.705 --> 00:02:35.145 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try the MathMagic practice, and explain your thinking to
someone.
